          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l21",
          "reminder": "Splitting at a modulus formula change allows each area to be calculated using its correct linear pieces."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the graph is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> throughout <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. The equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, which is outside this case.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, the two triangular areas give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, with roots <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Only the smaller lies in this interval.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, the graph is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> throughout, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The equation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, which is allowed.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> There are two distinct solutions, answer C. The boundary values <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> were included in the middle case and neither solves the equation; the rejected larger quadratic root exceeds 2.</p>"
}
